var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/metrics/#Datasets-and-conventions","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"The stability metrics themselves are ResilienceCore's, re-exported here: resilience, reactivity, amplification_envelope, max_amplification, pulse_covariance, the median metrics, stochastic_invariability, the press sensitivities, and the simulated experiments (PerturbationOptions, global_mortality_tolerance, local_mortality_tolerances, press_response, removal_responses, extinction_tolerance). Their documentation is in ResilienceCore.jl. The experiments run on the assembled communities of FoodWebDynamics.jl, which implement ResilienceCore's community interface.\n\nThis page documents what is specific to the paper: the Jacobian of an assembled community, the conventions the published Dataset S1 actually used, and the dataset layout.","title":"Datasets and conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/metrics/#The-community's-Jacobian","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"","title":"The community's Jacobian"},{"category":"section","location":"api/metrics/#The-published-conventions","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"","title":"The published conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/metrics/#Datasets","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"","title":"Datasets"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.community_jacobian","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"community_jacobian(c::Community)\n\nThe Jacobian of the bioenergetic model at the assembled equilibrium of c, as ResilienceCore's ContinuousJacobian with the surviving species' biomasses as its equilibrium (so that the median metrics' default pulses are proportional to them).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.community_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.published_max_amplification","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"published_max_amplification(op::ContinuousJacobian; dt = 0.1)\n\n(Amax, tmax) as in Dataset S1: the maximum of the amplification envelope in the ∞-norm (maximum absolute row sum of exp(J t)) on a grid of step dt, but (1.0, 0.0) whenever the reactivity in the spectral norm is not positive. In the published data Amax = 1 exactly when R0 ≤ 0, so the gate uses the 2-norm reactivity R0 even though the envelope uses the ∞-norm. ResilienceCore's max_amplification(op; p = Inf) gates on the ∞-norm reactivity instead, which can differ when R0 ≤ 0 but the ∞-norm logarithmic norm is positive; this helper exists only to keep the published convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.published_max_amplification"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.published_pulse_covariance","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"published_pulse_covariance(c::Community)\n\nThe pulse covariance behind the published median metrics (MR0, MRinf, MAmax, Mtmax) of Dataset S1: Diagonal(w.^2) with w = c.B[1:N]. That is the first N entries of the biomass vector over all initial species (extinct ones at zero, in niche order; see FoodWebDynamics' niche_model), set against the N surviving species. The weights agree with pulse_covariance(biomass(c)) (ResilienceCore) only when no species went extinct during assembly. The misalignment reproduces the published data: in communities that kept every species the published median metrics match the aligned weights, with MR0 ≈ −0.76 and no amplification. After extinctions, MR0 drifts towards 0 and about a fifth of communities amplify, as in the published data; aligned weights give neither.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.published_pulse_covariance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.amplification_metrics","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"amplification_metrics(c::Community; convention = :published, dt = 0.1)\n\nThe six raw columns of community_record that depend on the convention, as a NamedTuple (Amax, tmax, MRinf, MR0, MAmax, Mtmax).\n\n:published reproduces Dataset S1. Amax and tmax come from the envelope in the ∞-norm (published_max_amplification), and the median metrics use published_pulse_covariance.\n:si follows the definitions of the SI: the spectral norm (max_amplification of ResilienceCore), and pulses proportional to the surviving species' own biomasses (pulse_covariance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.amplification_metrics"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.RAW_COLUMNS","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"RAW_COLUMNS\n\nThe 39 data columns of the raw Dataset S1/S2 (after the network identifier), in file order: 28 metric columns, then N, iniN, Nb, Napex, Ncan, Btot, B_IPR and the community's exponents a_i, a_j, h_i, h_j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.RAW_COLUMNS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.METRICS","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"METRICS\n\nThe labels of the 27 stability metrics, in the order of measures in the authors' code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.METRICS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.community_record","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"community_record([rng], c::Community; options = PerturbationOptions(), dt = 0.1,\n                 convention = :published)\n\nAll raw columns of Dataset S1 (RAW_COLUMNS) for one assembled community, as a NamedTuple. This runs every perturbation experiment, so it is the expensive step. convention selects how Amax, tmax and the four median metrics are computed (see amplification_metrics). :published reproduces Dataset S1, and :si follows the SI's definitions.\n\nThe global press (RM_p, L1DBRM_p) raises every species' mortality by options.press, as the local presses do. Ranked within each richness, the published RM_p agrees with the sum of the local responses (Spearman ρ ≈ 0.92), as this definition does, but its values are about 30 times larger. No stronger press tried (mortality ×2–×11, plant carrying capacity, all loss terms) reproduces both the size and the ranking. The scale does not affect the paper's rank-based analyses.\n\nThe simulation experiments are those of ResilienceCore (global_mortality_tolerance, press_response, removal_responses, extinction_tolerance, …), run through FoodWebDynamics' implementation of the community interface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.community_record"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.prepare_metrics","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"prepare_metrics(raw::DataFrame)\n\nThe 27 stability metrics (METRICS) from raw Dataset-S1-style columns, transformed as the authors' prepare_dataframe does so that larger values mean more stability: sign flips, absolute values, normalisation of absumS by N² and of Ext_half by N, and the secondary extinction counts N(1 − Robst). Returns a DataFrame with network (if present), the 27 metrics and N. On the authors' data this equals the publisher's Dataset S1/S2 column by column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.prepare_metrics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#DominguezGarcia2019.read_metrics","page":"Datasets and conventions","text":"read_metrics(path)\n\nRead a dataset of stability metrics and return it in the form of prepare_metrics: either a raw Dataset S1/S2 from the authors' GitHub deposit or one written by community_record (both have the column L1DBRMt_s), or the publisher's Dataset S1/S2 (LaTeX column labels, already transformed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.read_metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Analysis-of-correlations-among-metrics","page":"Analysis","text":"","title":"Analysis of correlations among metrics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.SizeCorrelations","page":"Analysis","text":"SizeCorrelations\n\nPer-size correlation structure of a metrics table: for each richness in sizes, the Spearman correlation matrix rho, the covariance of ranks rankcov and the covariance of mean/range-normalised values valuecov of the metrics in labels (arrays are metrics × metrics × sizes), and the number of communities n used at each size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.SizeCorrelations"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.size_correlations","page":"Analysis","text":"size_correlations(df; labels = METRICS, sizes = 5:99, max_sample = 100, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nCorrelations among the metrics labels of a table such as prepare_metrics returns, separately for the communities of each richness N in sizes, as in the authors' get_full_correlation_bysize (window width 0). At most max_sample communities per size are used, drawn with rng when there are more. The authors' loop covers N = 5–99.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.size_correlations"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.class_average","page":"Analysis","text":"class_average(sc::SizeCorrelations, lo, hi; field = :rho)\n\nThe element-wise mean and standard deviation over the sizes lo:hi (inclusive) of one of the per-size matrices (:rho, :rankcov or :valuecov), as the authors' get_av_corr_from_sdf and get_av_cov_from_sdf compute for the size classes small (5–15), medium (45–55) and large (85–95). Like pandas' mean() and std() there, undefined values (NaN, for a metric that is constant at some size) are skipped; an entry undefined at every size stays NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.class_average"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.SIZE_CLASSES","page":"Analysis","text":"SIZE_CLASSES\n\nThe three size classes of the paper, as name => (lo, hi) in the authors' processing order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.SIZE_CLASSES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.metric_network","page":"Analysis","text":"metric_network(R)\n\nThe weighted, unsigned network of metrics from a matrix of mean correlations R: edge weights |Rᵢⱼ| (the absolute value of the averaged correlation, as in the authors' code), no self-loops. An undefined correlation (NaN) gives no edge, as the authors' threshold test abs(mean) > threshold is false for NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.metric_network"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.modularity","page":"Analysis","text":"modularity(W, groups; resolution = 1.0)\n\nNewman's modularity of the partition groups (one label per node) of the weighted undirected network W.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.modularity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.modular_groups","page":"Analysis","text":"modular_groups(W; runs = 10, resolution = 1.0, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nGroups of nodes of the weighted network W that maximise modularity, by the Louvain method, keeping the best of runs randomised runs (the paper ran Gephi's implementation ten times). Returns (groups, Q), with groups numbered 1, 2, … in order of first appearance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.modular_groups"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.cophenetic","page":"Analysis","text":"cophenetic(h::Hclust)\n\nThe matrix of cophenetic distances of a hierarchical clustering: for each pair of leaves, the height at which they are first merged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.cophenetic"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.metric_dendrogram","page":"Analysis","text":"metric_dendrogram(R; distance = :profiles)\n\nAverage-linkage hierarchical clustering of the metrics from their mean correlation matrix R. distance = :profiles follows the authors' code: rows of |R| are treated as correlation profiles and compared by correlation distance (SciPy metric='correlation'). distance = :one_minus_rho follows the paper's text, d = 1 − ρ. Returns the Hclust and the cophenetic correlation against both the clustering distance and, as the authors' code computes it, the Euclidean distance between |R| profiles: (tree, c_clustering, c_code).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.metric_dendrogram"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.explained_variance","page":"Analysis","text":"explained_variance(Σ)\n\nFor each variable of the covariance matrix Σ, the share of the total variance it explains through its covariances, Σⱼ Σⱼᵢ² / (tr(Σ) Σᵢᵢ) (the authors' get_exp_var_ind), and its own share Σᵢᵢ / tr(Σ), as (exp_var, simple).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.explained_variance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.ellipsoid_volume","page":"Analysis","text":"ellipsoid_volume(Σ)\n\nThe volume of the covariance ellipsoid of Σ after scaling its eigenvalues to sum to one, as in the authors' calculate_HV_covellipsoid: the unit-ball volume times the product of the square roots of the scaled eigenvalues. Isotropic covariance gives the largest value (0.8061 in three dimensions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.ellipsoid_volume"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#DominguezGarcia2019.correlation_types","page":"Analysis","text":"correlation_types(sc::SizeCorrelations; threshold = 0.1)\n\nThe classification of each metric pair by how its correlation changes with community size (SI §1, Fig. S1), with the authors' size windows: small 5–14 and 5–15, medium 50–59 (the code's positional slice; 45–55 for the sign test), large 85–95. Returns a DataFrame with columns a, b and type, the panel letter \"A\" to \"G\" (G: unchanged), or \"-\" if unassigned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.correlation_types"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_one_community/#One-food-web-and-its-27-stability-metrics","page":"One food web and its 27 metrics","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nA niche-model web\nAssembly\nResponses to small pulses, from the Jacobian\nResponses to presses and extinctions, by simulation","title":"One food web and its 27 stability metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_one_community/#Overview","page":"One food web and its 27 metrics","text":"The authors’ simulation code is not public, so this package rebuilds it from SI §8–9 of Domínguez-García et al. (2019). The published dataset is used to settle what the text leaves open or gets wrong (see the project wiki: Simulator reconstruction and Calibration). This vignette follows one community through the whole procedure:\n\ndraw a niche-model web;\nrun the bioenergetic model to a steady state;\ncompute the 27 metrics, from the Jacobian and from simulated presses  and extinctions.\n\nusing DominguezGarcia2019\nusing FoodWebDynamics\nusing DataFrames, Statistics, Random, Printf\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_one_community/#A-niche-model-web","page":"One food web and its 27 metrics","text":"The niche model of Williams & Martinez (2000) places species on a niche axis and lets each eat the species within a range below it. The connectance is 0.15, as in the paper.\n\nrng = Xoshiro(42)\nweb = niche_model(rng, 30, 0.15)\nL = trophic_levels(web)\n(species = richness(web), connectance = round(connectance(web); digits = 3), basal = count(isbasal(web)),\n max_trophic_level = L === nothing ? missing : round(maximum(L); digits = 2))\n\n(species = 30, connectance = 0.154, basal = 4, max_trophic_level = missing)","title":"A niche-model web"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_one_community/#Assembly","page":"One food web and its 27 metrics","text":"assemble_community draws a web and the community’s allometric exponents, starts from random biomasses and integrates to a steady state. Species are removed when they fall below 10⁻⁶ of their body mass. The web is discarded if more than 10% of its species die, and plants left without consumers are then removed. Most draws are discarded. The status records why.\n\nattempts = Symbol[]\nc = nothing\nwhile c === nothing\n    global c, status = assemble_community(rng, 30)\n    push!(attempts, status)\nend\n(attempts = attempts, initial = c.initial_richness, final = richness(c))\n\n(attempts = [:ok], initial = 30, final = 27)\n\nB = biomass(c)\nalive = findall(c.model.alive)\nscatter(c.model.trophic_level[alive], B; yscale = :log10, group = ifelse.(c.model.basal[alive], \"plant\", \"animal\"),\n        xlabel = \"trophic level\", ylabel = \"equilibrium biomass\", size = (600, 380))\n\n(Image: )\n\nWithout the metabolic loss that SI §8 lists for them, plants sit close to their carrying capacity of 1. Only this choice reproduces the dataset’s total biomass per plant (0.98).","title":"Assembly"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_one_community/#Responses-to-small-pulses,-from-the-Jacobian","page":"One food web and its 27 metrics","text":"The Jacobian at equilibrium gives resilience (the slowest return rate) and reactivity (the fastest initial amplification). The amplification envelope A(t) = ‖exp(Jt)‖ is the largest displacement at time t of any unit pulse. The “median” versions average over pulses proportional to equilibrium biomass (Arnoldi et al. 2018).\n\nop = community_jacobian(c)\nC = pulse_covariance(B)\nts = 0:0.1:40\nplot(ts, [amplification_envelope(op, t) for t in ts]; label = \"A(t), worst pulse\", lw = 2, yscale = :log10,\n     xlabel = \"time\", ylabel = \"displacement relative to the pulse\", size = (600, 380))\nplot!(ts, [median_displacement(op, C, t) for t in ts]; label = \"median pulse\", lw = 2)\n\n(Image: )\n\nAmax, tmax = max_amplification(op)\n(resilience = resilience(op), reactivity = reactivity(op), Amax = Amax, tmax = tmax,\n median_reactivity = median_reactivity(op, C), stochastic_invariability = stochastic_invariability(op),\n sensitivity = press_sensitivity(op))\n\n(resilience = 0.027373151276825505, reactivity = 0.4707792320817974, Amax = 2.5862447957697587, tmax = 9.3, median_reactivity = -0.6753929845020011, stochastic_invariability = 0.004499060622894939, sensitivity = 340.9024950559039)\n\nThe equilibrium is reactive: some pulses grow by a factor of Amax before they decay. A pulse spread over species in proportion to their biomass decays from the start.","title":"Responses to small pulses, from the Jacobian"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_one_community/#Responses-to-presses-and-extinctions,-by-simulation","page":"One food web and its 27 metrics","text":"Tolerance to mortality is the smallest increase in mortality that causes an extinction. It is measured for all species at once (global) and for each species alone (local). Raising every species’ mortality by 10% and removing species one at a time give the resistance and sensitivity metrics.\n\nfactors = 1.0:0.25:4\nresp = [press_response(c, fill(f, length(state(c)))) for f in factors]   # every species' mortality × f\nplot(factors, [100 * r[1] for r in resp]; lw = 2, label = \"loss of total biomass (%)\",\n     xlabel = \"mortality multiplier (all species)\", size = (600, 380))\nplot!(factors, [r[3] for r in resp]; lw = 2, label = \"extinctions\", seriestype = :steppost)\nvline!([global_mortality_tolerance(c)]; label = \"TM (first extinction)\", ls = :dash)\n\n(Image: )\n\ncommunity_record runs every experiment and returns the 39 raw columns of the authors’ Dataset S1. prepare_metrics then turns them into the 27 metrics, each signed so that larger means more stable.\n\nrec = community_record(rng, c)\nm = prepare_metrics(DataFrame([rec]))\nDataFrame(metric = METRICS, value = [m[1, k] for k in METRICS])\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">27×2 DataFrame</div><div style = \"float: right; font-style: italic;\">2 rows omitted</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow metric value\n String Float64\n1 R0 -0.470779\n2 Is 0.00449906\n3 Amax -5.57401\n4 tmax -6.8\n5 Rinf 0.0273732\n6 MR0 0.675282\n7 MAmax -1.0\n8 Mtmax -0.0\n9 MRinf 0.0368317\n10 S -340.902\n11 \\<s_ij\\> -1.64206\n12 TM 4.401\n13 \\<TM\\> 24.6333\n⋮ ⋮ ⋮\n16 TE 0.403704\n17 maxCE -7.26923\n18 RM -0.00224119\n19 \\<RM\\> -8.32026e-5\n20 maxRM -0.00079769\n21 SM -0.0062326\n22 \\<SM\\> -0.000675949\n23 maxSM -0.00488788\n24 \\<RE\\> -0.0403705\n25 maxRE -0.157127\n26 \\<SE\\> -0.102798\n27 maxSE -0.459017\n\n</div>","title":"Responses to presses and extinctions, by simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_simulated_ensemble/#End-to-end:-a-simulated-ensemble-against-the-published-one","page":"End to end: a simulated ensemble","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nThe metrics, size by size\nThe analysis on simulated communities\nHow correlations change with size","title":"End to end: a simulated ensemble against the published one"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_simulated_ensemble/#Overview","page":"End to end: a simulated ensemble","text":"This vignette closes the loop. It compares an ensemble of communities simulated with this package (scripts/simulate_dataset.jl) with the authors’ Dataset S1/S2, first metric by metric and then through the paper’s analysis: correlations by size class, modular groups, and how correlations change with community size.\n\nusing DominguezGarcia2019\nusing CSV, DataFrames, Statistics, Random, Printf\nusing Plots\ndata = joinpath(pkgdir(DominguezGarcia2019), \"data\")\nsim_raw = CSV.read(joinpath(data, \"simulated\", \"ensemble_v4.csv\"), DataFrame)\npub_raw = CSV.read(joinpath(data, \"dominguez-garcia2019\", \"Dataset_S2.csv\"), DataFrame)\n(simulated = nrow(sim_raw), published = nrow(pub_raw),\n median_seconds_per_community = median(sim_raw.seconds))\n\n(simulated = 10000, published = 9380, median_seconds_per_community = 6.38)\n\nEach simulated community was drawn with initial richness uniform on 5–110 and its own random seed (Sim<seed>), so every row can be regenerated.\n\nThe raw columns follow the published convention (community_record, convention = :published). Amax and tmax come from the ∞-norm of the propagator, and the four median metrics use the published pulse weights, which are misaligned after extinctions (published_pulse_covariance). The SI’s versions of these six columns are kept as *_si (see lab/wiki/analysis/published-conventions.md).","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_simulated_ensemble/#The-metrics,-size-by-size","page":"End to end: a simulated ensemble","text":"bins = 5:10:105\nbinned(df, col) = [let v = df[(df.N .>= b) .& (df.N .< b + 10), col]\n                       isempty(v) ? NaN : median(v)        # the published data stop at N = 99\n                   end for b in bins]\ncols = [:Rinf, :R0, :Amax, :tmax, :MR0, :Is, :TM_p, :minTM_i, :av_TM_i, :Ext_half, :RM_p, :Robst, :Btot, :Nb]\nplot([plot(bins .+ 5, binned(pub_raw, c); label = \"published\", lw = 2, title = string(c), titlefontsize = 9,\n           legend = c === :Rinf ? :topright : false) for c in cols]...; layout = (4, 4), size = (950, 800))\nfor (k, c) in enumerate(cols)\n    plot!(bins .+ 5, binned(sim_raw, c); subplot = k, label = \"simulated\", lw = 2)\nend\ncurrent()\n\n(Image: )\n\nMedians by richness, in the raw columns of the dataset (before the sign changes of the analysis). The calibration targets were total biomass and the number of plants. The other panels are independent checks of the reconstructed simulator.","title":"The metrics, size by size"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_simulated_ensemble/#The-analysis-on-simulated-communities","page":"End to end: a simulated ensemble","text":"The published Dataset S2 has 100 communities at each richness. The 10,000 simulated communities give about as many per richness (the median is printed below), so correlations at a single richness are about as noisy in both; class averages pool 11 sizes.\n\nsim = prepare_metrics(sim_raw)\npub = read_metrics(joinpath(data, \"dominguez-garcia2019\", \"Dataset_S2.csv\"))\nanalysed = 5 .<= sim.N .<= 99\nshare_varying(v) = 1 - maximum(count(==(x), v) for x in unique(v)) / length(v)\nvariation = DataFrame(metric = METRICS,\n                      simulated = [share_varying(sim[analysed, l]) for l in METRICS],\n                      published = [share_varying(pub[!, l]) for l in METRICS])\nfilter(r -> min(r.simulated, r.published) < 0.5, variation)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">2×3 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow metric simulated published\n String Float64 Float64\n1 MAmax 0.199507 0.221855\n2 Mtmax 0.199507 0.221855\n\n</div>\n\nOnly the median maximal amplification and its time take few distinct values: a median pulse either grows (MAmax \\> 1) or the metrics are exactly (1, 0). The published communities amplify in 22% of cases; ours do in 1944 of 9744. With the SI’s aligned weights (MAmax_si) almost none do (3), so the two metrics would be constant. The comparison below uses every metric that varies in at least 1% of communities in both datasets.\n\nlabels = filter(r -> min(r.simulated, r.published) >= 0.01, variation).metric\nsc_sim = size_correlations(sim; labels, rng = Xoshiro(2019))   # up to 100 communities per richness\nsc_pub = size_correlations(pub; labels, rng = Xoshiro(2019))\n(metrics = length(labels), sizes = length(sc_sim.sizes), median_per_size = median(sc_sim.n))\n\n(metrics = 27, sizes = 95, median_per_size = 100.0)\n\nupper(R) = [R[i, j] for j in axes(R, 2) for i in axes(R, 1) if i < j]\nplots = map(SIZE_CLASSES) do (name, (lo, hi))\n    Rs, _ = class_average(sc_sim, lo, hi)\n    Rp, _ = class_average(sc_pub, lo, hi)\n    x, y = upper(Rp), upper(Rs)\n    scatter(x, y; ms = 2, alpha = 0.6, label = false, xlabel = \"published ρ\", ylabel = \"simulated ρ\",\n            title = @sprintf(\"%s: r = %.2f\", name, cor(x, y)), xlim = (-1, 1), ylim = (-1, 1), aspect_ratio = 1)\n    plot!([-1, 1], [-1, 1]; label = false, color = :grey)\nend\nplot(plots...; layout = (1, 3), size = (950, 340))\n\n(Image: )\n\nEach point is one of the 300 pairs of metrics: its mean correlation in a size class in the published data against the simulated data. Points on the diagonal mean the simulator reproduces that relationship.\n\nfunction groups_table(sc, lo, hi)\n    R, _ = class_average(sc, lo, hi)\n    g, Q = modular_groups(metric_network(R); runs = 50, rng = Xoshiro(1))\n    return g, Q\nend\nrand_index(a, b) = mean((a[i] == a[j]) == (b[i] == b[j]) for i in eachindex(a) for j in eachindex(a) if i < j)\nfor (name, (lo, hi)) in SIZE_CLASSES\n    gs, Qs = groups_table(sc_sim, lo, hi)\n    gp, Qp = groups_table(sc_pub, lo, hi)\n    @printf(\"%-6s Q simulated %.3f, published %.3f; Rand index of the partitions %.2f\\n\", name, Qs, Qp,\n            rand_index(gs, gp))\n    foreach(k -> println(\"   \", join(sc_sim.labels[gs .== k], \" \")), 1:maximum(gs))\nend\n\nsmall  Q simulated 0.135, published 0.147; Rand index of the partitions 0.97\n   R0 Is Amax tmax Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n   MR0 RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\n   MAmax Mtmax\nlarge  Q simulated 0.187, published 0.207; Rand index of the partitions 1.00\n   R0 Amax tmax MR0 MAmax Mtmax\n   Is Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n   RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\nmedium Q simulated 0.178, published 0.177; Rand index of the partitions 0.88\n   R0 Amax tmax MR0 MAmax Mtmax\n   Is Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n   RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\n\nThe Rand index is the share of pairs of metrics on which the two partitions agree (together in both, or apart in both).","title":"The analysis on simulated communities"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_simulated_ensemble/#How-correlations-change-with-size","page":"End to end: a simulated ensemble","text":"t_sim = combine(groupby(correlation_types(sc_sim), :type), nrow => :simulated)\nt_pub = combine(groupby(correlation_types(sc_pub), :type), nrow => :published)\nsort(outerjoin(t_pub, t_sim; on = :type), :type)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">7×3 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow type published simulated\n String Int64? Int64?\n1 A 7 2\n2 B 8 19\n3 C 7 3\n4 D 5 5\n5 E 64 48\n6 F 105 102\n7 G 155 172\n\n</div>\n\nThe classes are those of SI §1, Fig. S1: G unchanged; E and F strengthen or weaken from small to medium communities, then stay constant; B and C keep strengthening or weakening; A and D change sign.","title":"How correlations change with size"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_early_response/#The-early-response-to-pulse:-median-metrics-and-an-indexing-artefact","page":"The early response to pulse: an indexing artefact","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nMedian metrics and their pulse weights\nEvidence from the published data alone\nThe same communities, computed both ways\nWhat this does to the stability dimensions\nCaveats","title":"The early response to pulse: median metrics and an indexing artefact"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_early_response/#Overview","page":"The early response to pulse: an indexing artefact","text":"Domínguez-García et al. (2019) group 27 stability metrics into three “dimensions” of stability. One of them, the early response to pulse, is built around three median metrics:\n\nthe median reactivity MR0;\nthe median maximal amplification MAmax;\nthe time at which it is reached, Mtmax.\n\nThis vignette shows that MAmax and Mtmax vary between communities, and so can form a group at all, only because of a bookkeeping error in how the median metrics were weighted. The evidence comes first from the published data alone and then from simulations computed both ways.\n\nusing DominguezGarcia2019\nusing FoodWebDynamics\nusing CSV, DataFrames, Statistics, Random, Printf\nusing Plots\ndata = joinpath(pkgdir(DominguezGarcia2019), \"data\")\npub = CSV.read(joinpath(data, \"dominguez-garcia2019\", \"Dataset_S2.csv\"), DataFrame)\nsim = CSV.read(joinpath(data, \"simulated\", \"ensemble_v4.csv\"), DataFrame)\nsim = sim[5 .<= sim.N .<= 99, :]\n(published = nrow(pub), simulated = nrow(sim))\n\n(published = 9380, simulated = 9744)","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_early_response/#Median-metrics-and-their-pulse-weights","page":"The early response to pulse: an indexing artefact","text":"The median metrics average the response of the linearised dynamics, with Jacobian J, over random pulses whose covariance is C (Arnoldi et al. 2018). The SI follows that paper and takes each species’ pulse proportional to its equilibrium biomass, C = diag(B²). The median reactivity is then\n\nMR_0 = fracoperatornameTr(C J)operatornameTr C\n\nThe published values are reproduced by a different C (see the package documentation of published_pulse_covariance). It takes the biomass vector over all initial species (extinct ones at zero, species in niche order), truncates it to its first N entries, and uses those as the weights of the N survivors. When no species died during assembly the two coincide. After an extinction, survivors receive other species’ biomasses, or zeros.\n\nfunction community_with_losses(rng, S, minlost)    # first viable draw that lost species\n    while true\n        c, _ = assemble_community(rng, S)\n        c !== nothing && c.initial_richness - richness(c) ≥ minlost && return c\n    end\nend\nc = community_with_losses(Xoshiro(7), 30, 3)\nop = community_jacobian(c)\nN = richness(c)\nweights = DataFrame(species = findall(c.model.alive), own_biomass = biomass(c),\n                    published_weight = c.B[1:N])\nfilter(r -> r.own_biomass != r.published_weight, weights)   # survivors whose weight is wrong\n\n┌ Warning: Verbosity toggle: max_iters \n│  Interrupted. Larger maxiters is needed. If you are using an integrator for non-stiff ODEs or an automatic switching algorithm (the default), you may want to consider using a method for stiff equations. See the solver pages for more details (e.g. https://docs.sciml.ai/DiffEqDocs/stable/solvers/ode_solve/#Stiff-Problems).\n└ @ DiffEqBase ~/.julia/packages/DiffEqBase/WgyOh/src/check_error.jl:44\n┌ Warning: Verbosity toggle: max_iters \n│  Interrupted. Larger maxiters is needed. If you are using an integrator for non-stiff ODEs or an automatic switching algorithm (the default), you may want to consider using a method for stiff equations. See the solver pages for more details (e.g. https://docs.sciml.ai/DiffEqDocs/stable/solvers/ode_solve/#Stiff-Problems).\n└ @ DiffEqBase ~/.julia/packages/DiffEqBase/WgyOh/src/check_error.jl:44\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">11×3 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow species own_biomass published_weight\n Int64 Float64 Float64\n1 18 0.58296 0.0\n2 19 0.00468883 0.58296\n3 20 0.00192724 0.00468883\n4 21 0.583215 0.00192724\n5 22 0.00282098 0.583215\n6 23 0.00527902 0.00282098\n7 24 0.00282447 0.00527902\n8 25 0.0105302 0.00282447\n9 26 0.000426869 0.0105302\n10 27 0.00205099 0.000426869\n11 30 1.60977 0.00205099\n\n</div>\n\n(species_lost = c.initial_richness - N,\n MR0_si = median_reactivity(op, pulse_covariance(biomass(c))),\n MR0_published = median_reactivity(op, published_pulse_covariance(c)))\n\n(species_lost = 3, MR0_si = -0.5279977995913107, MR0_published = -0.7077880130814678)","title":"Median metrics and their pulse weights"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_early_response/#Evidence-from-the-published-data-alone","page":"The early response to pulse: an indexing artefact","text":"Communities that lost no species during assembly are unaffected by the misalignment. If median amplification were a property of the communities, it would vary smoothly with how many species they lost. Instead it switches on at the first extinction.\n\nlost_class(l) = l == 0 ? \"0\" : l == 1 ? \"1\" : l ≤ 4 ? \"2–4\" : \"≥5\"\nby_lost(df) = combine(groupby(transform(df, [:iniN, :N] => ByRow((i, n) -> lost_class(i - n)) => :lost), :lost),\n                      nrow => :communities, :MAmax => (x -> mean(x .> 1 + 1e-12)) => :share_amplifying,\n                      :MR0 => median => :median_MR0)\nsort(by_lost(pub), :lost)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">4×4 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow lost communities share_amplifying median_MR0\n String Int64 Float64 Float64\n1 0 899 0.0144605 -0.744172\n2 1 1287 0.231546 -0.525975\n3 2–4 3144 0.243003 -0.449234\n4 ≥5 4050 0.248395 -0.340803\n\n</div>\n\nIn Dataset S2, communities that lost no species almost never amplify a median pulse. With one or more extinctions, about a quarter do, regardless of how many species were lost. The median reactivity jumps in the same way.","title":"Evidence from the published data alone"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_early_response/#The-same-communities,-computed-both-ways","page":"The early response to pulse: an indexing artefact","text":"Our ensemble stores every community’s median metrics under both weightings. The raw columns follow the published convention; the *_si columns follow the SI.\n\nsi = copy(sim)\nfor col in (:Amax, :tmax, :MRinf, :MR0, :MAmax, :Mtmax)\n    si[!, col] = si[!, Symbol(col, \"_si\")]\nend\nlost = sim.iniN .- sim.N\nclasses = [\"0\", \"1\", \"2–4\", \"≥5\"]\nshare(df) = [mean(df.MAmax[lost_class.(df.iniN .- df.N) .== k] .> 1 + 1e-12) for k in classes]\nplot(classes, [share(pub), share(sim), share(si)]; marker = :circle, lw = 2,\n     label = [\"published (Dataset S2)\" \"ours, published weights\" \"ours, SI weights\"],\n     xlabel = \"species lost during assembly\", ylabel = \"share with MAmax > 1\", ylim = (0, 0.35),\n     legend = :topleft, size = (650, 380))\n\n(Image: )\n\nWith the published weighting, our simulations reproduce the switch. With the SI’s weighting, the number of communities amplifying a median pulse falls to 3 out of 9744, so MAmax and Mtmax are effectively constant.","title":"The same communities, computed both ways"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_early_response/#What-this-does-to-the-stability-dimensions","page":"The early response to pulse: an indexing artefact","text":"The paper’s groups come from Louvain modularity on the network of metrics, whose edge weights are the absolute Spearman correlations averaged over a size class. A constant metric has no defined correlations, so it cannot belong to any group.\n\nsc = Dict(\"published\" => size_correlations(read_metrics(joinpath(data, \"dominguez-garcia2019\", \"Dataset_S2.csv\"))),\n          \"ours, published weights\" => size_correlations(prepare_metrics(sim)),\n          \"ours, SI weights\" => size_correlations(prepare_metrics(si)))\nfunction groups(s, lo, hi)\n    R, _ = class_average(s, lo, hi)\n    g, _ = modular_groups(metric_network(R); runs = 50, rng = Xoshiro(1))\n    return [join(s.labels[g .== k], \" \") for k in 1:maximum(g)]\nend\nfor name in (\"published\", \"ours, published weights\", \"ours, SI weights\")\n    println(name)\n    foreach(x -> println(\"    \", x), groups(sc[name], 85, 95))\nend\n\npublished\n    R0 Amax tmax MR0 MAmax Mtmax\n    Is Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n    RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\nours, published weights\n    R0 Amax tmax MR0 MAmax Mtmax\n    Is Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n    RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\nours, SI weights\n    R0 Amax tmax\n    Is Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n    MR0 RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\n    MAmax\n    Mtmax\n\nIn large communities (85–95 species) the published data and our simulations with the published weights give the same three groups. The early-response group holds R0, Amax, tmax, MR0, MAmax and Mtmax. With the SI’s weights, MAmax and Mtmax are isolated, and MR0 joins the sensitivities to press. Of the paper’s three dimensions, distance to threshold and sensitivity to press survive the correction. The early response to pulse reduces to the extremal pulse metrics R0, Amax and tmax.","title":"What this does to the stability dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_early_response/#Caveats","page":"The early response to pulse: an indexing artefact","text":"The mechanism is inferred. The authors’ simulation code is not public. The truncation reproduces the published distributions and their dependence on species lost, but it is inferred from the data, not read from their code.\nThe published evidence needs no simulator. The switch at the first extinction comes from the published data alone. Only the comparison of groups under the SI’s weighting depends on our reconstruction.\nWhy median pulses rarely amplify here. In these communities, median pulses scaled by biomass are dominated by plants. Plants hold most of the biomass and regulate themselves strongly, so median amplification is rare under the SI’s definition. This is a property of the model family, not a general statement about median amplification.","title":"Caveats"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#Observed-food-webs:-the-27-metrics-on-GATEWAy-topologies","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nThe data\nTaxa must be merged into trophic species\nMeasured body masses starve the consumers\nThe observed webs under the bioenergetic model\nMetric values: observed against niche-model webs\nCorrelation structure\nCaveats\nReferences","title":"Observed food webs: the 27 metrics on GATEWAy topologies"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#Overview","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"The stability dimensions of Domínguez-García et al. (2019) come from niche-model food webs. A common criticism is that correlations among metrics then describe that ensemble of webs, not real communities. This vignette swaps the network: the same bioenergetic dynamics and the same 27 metrics run on observed topologies from GATEWAy v1.0 (Brose et al. 2019).\n\nThe data: 290 observed webs with binary feeding links and body masses, downloaded when the vignette is rendered.\nTwo preparations are needed:\ntaxa with identical prey and predators must be merged into trophic species;\nthe model’s own masses must replace the measured ones.\nEach is shown before it is used.\nThe comparison: metric values and the correlation structure of observed webs against the niche-model ensemble of the simulated-ensemble vignette, judged against the noise from splitting the observed webs in half.\n\nusing DominguezGarcia2019\nusing FoodWebDynamics\nusing CSV, DataFrames, Statistics, Random, Printf\nusing Plots\nusing Downloads, SHA, p7zip_jll\nusing LinearAlgebra: BLAS\nBLAS.set_num_threads(1)       # Julia threads run the webs; BLAS threads would oversubscribe\ndata = joinpath(pkgdir(DominguezGarcia2019), \"data\")","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#The-data","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"GATEWAy v1.0 is published by iDiv under CC BY 4.0 (doi:10.25829/IDIV.283-3-756). It is too large to ship with the package (a 96 MB table), so it is downloaded once and cached.\n\nWhere: ~/.cache/DominguezGarcia2019/gateway, or the directory in ENV[\"GATEWAY_CACHE\"]. To use a copy you already have, set ENV[\"GATEWAY_CSV\"] to its path.\nCheck: the table’s SHA-256 is verified. The zip itself is rebuilt by the server on each request, so its checksum changes and is not checked.\nOffline: the vignette stops with a message saying how to supply the file.\n\nconst GATEWAY_URL = \"https://idata.idiv.de/ddm/Data/DownloadZip/283?version=756\"\nconst GATEWAY_FILE = \"283_2_FoodWebDataBase_2018_12_10.csv\"\nconst GATEWAY_SHA256 = \"0862d9f94bd144cb33a635e905bfab186236b122a773236d2521970bf2e9032f\"\n\nfunction gateway_csv()\n    path = get(ENV, \"GATEWAY_CSV\", \"\")\n    if isempty(path)\n        root = get(ENV, \"XDG_CACHE_HOME\", joinpath(homedir(), \".cache\"))\n        cache = get(ENV, \"GATEWAY_CACHE\", joinpath(root, \"DominguezGarcia2019\", \"gateway\"))\n        path = joinpath(cache, GATEWAY_FILE)\n        if !isfile(path)\n            mkpath(cache)\n            zip = joinpath(cache, \"gateway.zip\")\n            # the server assembles the zip on request and sends nothing for about 30 s\n            dl = Downloads.Downloader()\n            dl.easy_hook = (easy, _) -> Downloads.Curl.setopt(easy, Downloads.Curl.CURLOPT_LOW_SPEED_TIME, 300)\n            try\n                Downloads.download(GATEWAY_URL, zip; downloader = dl, timeout = 900)\n            catch err\n                error(\"Could not download GATEWAy from $GATEWAY_URL: \", first(sprint(showerror, err), 200),\n                      \"\\nDownload it by hand, extract $GATEWAY_FILE and set ENV[\\\"GATEWAY_CSV\\\"] to its path.\")\n            end\n            run(`$(p7zip_jll.p7zip()) e $zip $GATEWAY_FILE -o$cache -y -bso0 -bsp0`)\n            rm(zip)\n        end\n    end\n    h = bytes2hex(open(sha256, path))\n    h == GATEWAY_SHA256 || error(\"$path has SHA-256 $h, not that of the GATEWAy v1.0 link table\")\n    return path\nend\n\nread_gateway (from FoodWebDynamics) builds one web per foodweb.name. It strips names, drops exact duplicate links, takes a node’s mass as the geometric mean over its links (masses are recorded per link and life stage) and keeps detritus and other non-living resources as basal nodes.\n\ntaxa_webs = read_gateway(gateway_csv())\ncombine(groupby(DataFrame(ecosystem = [w.ecosystem for w in taxa_webs],\n                          taxa = [richness(w.web) for w in taxa_webs],\n                          nonliving = [count(.!w.living) for w in taxa_webs]), :ecosystem),\n        nrow => :webs, :taxa => median => :median_taxa, :nonliving => (x -> mean(x .> 0)) => :share_with_detritus)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">5×4 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow ecosystem webs median_taxa sharewithdetritus\n String Int64 Float64 Float64\n1 terrestrial aboveground 21 97.0 0.285714\n2 marine 134 28.0 0.0447761\n3 terrestrial belowground 52 122.5 0.942308\n4 lakes 55 37.0 0.781818\n5 streams 28 83.5 0.821429\n\n</div>","title":"The data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#Taxa-must-be-merged-into-trophic-species","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"In the bioenergetic model a species’ parameters follow from its links and trophic level. Two taxa with the same prey and the same predators are therefore identical. If both survive, the community can shift biomass from one to the other at no cost: the Jacobian has an eigenvalue 0, resilience is 0, and the sensitivity and variance metrics diverge. An earlier run on the published nodes found this in over a quarter of the converged communities.\n\ntrophic_species merges such taxa, repeating until no two nodes share both sets. This is also the unit Williams & Martinez (2000) used to compare the niche model with observed webs.\n\nwebs = trophic_species.(taxa_webs)\nkept = [richness(t.web) / richness(w.web) for (t, w) in zip(webs, taxa_webs)]\nusable = [k for (k, w) in enumerate(webs) if richness(w.web) ≤ 120 && trophic_levels(w.web) !== nothing]\n(webs_with_redundant_taxa = mean(kept .< 1), median_share_of_nodes_kept = median(kept),\n usable_webs = length(usable))\n\n(webs_with_redundant_taxa = 0.993103448275862, median_share_of_nodes_kept = 0.6909814323607427, usable_webs = 259)\n\nRedundancy is the rule. Almost every web has taxa with identical links, and aggregation keeps about two thirds of the nodes.\nUsable webs have at most 120 trophic species (the largest niche-model webs have 110 initial species) and defined trophic levels (a web whose consumers form a closed loop with no path to a basal node has none).","title":"Taxa must be merged into trophic species"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#Measured-body-masses-starve-the-consumers","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"GATEWAy records body masses, so the obvious choice is to use them. Under the paper’s rate scalings that fails:\n\nthe attack rate scales as aᵢⱼ ∝ mᵢ^0.7 and the handling time as hᵢⱼ ∝ mᵢ^−0.6 mⱼ^0.5;\na consumer’s maximum intake per unit biomass is about 1/(hᵢⱼ mᵢ) ∝ mᵢ^−0.4 mⱼ^−0.5;\nits metabolic loss scales as mᵢ^−0.25.\n\nSo intake falls behind metabolism as mass grows. In niche-model webs masses are Z^(L−1) with Z = 1.5, a few units at most, and the gap is small. Observed consumers are far heavier relative to their producers. Below, each consumer’s mass is divided by the geometric mean mass of its web’s living producers (relative_masses), over the usable webs that record a mass for every consumer.\n\nratios = reduce(vcat, [m[.!isbasal(webs[k].web)] for k in usable for m in (relative_masses(webs[k]),) if m !== nothing])\nq = quantile(ratios, [0.1, 0.5, 0.9])\nsci(x) = (e = floor(Int, log10(x)); string(round(Int, x / 10.0^e), \"×10\", map(c -> \"⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹\"[nextind(\"⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹\", 0, c - '0' + 1)], string(e))))\n@printf(\"consumer mass / producer mass over %d consumers: 10%% %.3g, median %.3g, 90%% %.3g\\n\", length(ratios), q...)\nhistogram(log10.(ratios); bins = 60, label = \"observed consumers\", xlabel = \"log₁₀ mass relative to producers\",\n          ylabel = \"consumers\", size = (650, 330))\nvspan!([0, log10(1.5^4)]; alpha = 0.2, label = \"niche-model masses, trophic levels 1–5\")\n\nconsumer mass / producer mass over 4180 consumers: 10% 0.757, median 66.2, 90% 2.07e+05\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe median consumer is 66 times heavier than its producers, and one in ten is more than 2×10⁵ times heavier. Niche-model masses stay below 1.5⁴ ≈ 5.\n\nA pilot makes the point dynamically. Webs of up to 40 trophic species with a mass for every consumer are run once with measured masses and once with the model’s masses, with the same draw of exponents and initial biomasses.\n\nconst PARAMS = BioenergeticParameters()\nconst EMPTY = NamedTuple{Tuple(RAW_COLUMNS)}(ntuple(_ -> NaN, length(RAW_COLUMNS)))\n\n# Surviving top consumers with the same surviving prey and the same mass are exactly neutral even\n# after aggregation (extinctions can remove the links that told them apart). Merging them into one\n# species with their summed biomass is exact, because their growth is linear in their own biomass.\n# (As in scripts/observed_webs.jl.)\nfunction merge_neutral!(model, B)\n    A, alive = model.web.A, model.alive\n    top = [i for i in findall(alive) if !model.basal[i] && !any(alive[k] && A[k, i] for k in eachindex(alive))]\n    merged = 0\n    for (a, i) in enumerate(top), j in top[(a + 1):end]\n        (alive[i] && alive[j] && model.mass[i] == model.mass[j]) || continue\n        if all(A[i, k] == A[j, k] for k in eachindex(alive) if alive[k])\n            B[i] += B[j]\n            kill!(model, B, j)\n            merged += 1\n        end\n    end\n    return merged\nend\n\n# One community on observed web k, replicate r: its own draw of allometric exponents and initial\n# biomasses (the seeds of scripts/observed_webs.jl), no survival filter, status recorded.\nfunction run_web(k, r; measured = false, metrics = true, seed = 20190000)\n    w = webs[k]\n    rng = Xoshiro(seed + 1000k + r)\n    S = richness(w.web)\n    model = FoodWebModel(w.web, PARAMS, draw_exponents(rng, PARAMS); mass = measured ? relative_masses(w) : nothing)\n    B, ok = settle!(model, 0.05 .+ 0.95 .* rand(rng, S); options = SteadyStateOptions(tmax = 1e5))\n    nd = richness(model)\n    iso = isolated_plants(model)\n    isempty(iso) || kill!(model, B, iso)\n    merged = ok ? merge_neutral!(model, B) : 0\n    status = !ok ? \"not converged\" : richness(model) < 3 ? \"collapsed\" : \"ok\"\n    rec = EMPTY\n    if metrics && status == \"ok\"\n        try\n            # the SI's median weights: the published ones assume niche-ordered species\n            rec = community_record(rng, Community(model, B, S, nd); convention = :si)\n            rec.Rinf > 1e-9 || (status, rec = \"neutral\", EMPTY)\n        catch err\n            status = \"error\"\n        end\n    end\n    info = (network = \"GW$(k)_$r\", web = w.name, ecosystem = w.ecosystem, replicate = r, nodes = S,\n            survivors = richness(model), status = status, merged = merged)\n    return merge(info, rec)\nend\n\nfunction run_all(jobs; kwargs...)\n    out = Vector{Any}(undef, length(jobs))\n    Threads.@threads :dynamic for i in eachindex(jobs)\n        out[i] = run_web(jobs[i]...; kwargs...)\n    end\n    return DataFrame(out)\nend\n\npilot = [k for k in usable if richness(webs[k].web) ≤ 40 && relative_masses(webs[k]) !== nothing]\nboth = vcat(insertcols!(run_all([(k, 1) for k in pilot]; measured = true, metrics = false), :masses => \"measured\"),\n            insertcols!(run_all([(k, 1) for k in pilot]; metrics = false), :masses => \"model, Z^(L−1)\"))\ncombine(groupby(both, :masses), nrow => :webs, :status => (s -> mean(s .== \"not converged\")) => :not_converged,\n        [:survivors, :nodes, :status] => ((n, s, st) -> median((n ./ s)[st .!= \"not converged\"])) => :median_share_surviving,\n        [:survivors, :status] => ((n, st) -> mean(n[st .!= \"not converged\"] .< 3)) => :share_below_3_species)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">2×5 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow masses webs not_converged mediansharesurviving sharebelow3_species\n String Int64 Float64 Float64 Float64\n1 measured 183 0.224044 0.285714 0.204225\n2 model, Z^(L−1) 183 0.245902 0.684659 0.00724638\n\n</div>\n\nWith measured masses most consumers starve. Of the communities that settle, the median keeps under a third of its species, against two thirds with the model’s masses, and a fifth fall below 3 species. Oscillations are about as common either way. Using measured masses would need a parameterisation in which maximum ingestion scales with metabolism (Brose et al. 2006). Here the observed topology is combined with the model’s masses, so only the network changes.","title":"Measured body masses starve the consumers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#The-observed-webs-under-the-bioenergetic-model","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"Every usable web is run three times, each with its own draw of exponents and initial biomasses. There is no survival filter: an observed web is not a random draw to accept or reject. Each community is recorded with a status:\n\nok: a stable equilibrium with at least 3 species;\nnot converged: no steady state within t = 10⁵ (typically sustained oscillations);\ncollapsed: fewer than 3 species left;\nneutral: a zero eigenvalue remains after merging neutral top consumers.\n\nt_run = @elapsed obs = run_all([(k, r) for k in usable for r in 1:3])\n@printf(\"%d communities in %.1f min on %d threads\\n\", nrow(obs), t_run / 60, Threads.nthreads())\neco = combine(groupby(obs, :ecosystem), nrow => :communities, :status => (s -> mean(s .== \"ok\")) => :share_ok,\n              :merged => (m -> sum(m .> 0)) => :with_merges)\npush!(eco, (\"all\", nrow(obs), mean(obs.status .== \"ok\"), sum(obs.merged .> 0)))\n\n777 communities in 10.1 min on 4 threads\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">6×4 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow ecosystem communities share_ok with_merges\n String Int64 Float64 Int64\n1 terrestrial aboveground 51 0.882353 0\n2 marine 354 0.714689 56\n3 terrestrial belowground 123 0.439024 17\n4 lakes 165 0.824242 5\n5 streams 84 0.833333 5\n6 all 777 0.718147 83\n\n</div>\n\ncombine(groupby(obs, :status), nrow => :communities)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">3×2 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow status communities\n String Int64\n1 ok 558\n2 not converged 217\n3 collapsed 2\n\n</div>\n\nAlmost three quarters reach a stable equilibrium. Nearly all the rest never settle; almost none collapse.\nEcosystems differ. Terrestrial aboveground, lake and stream webs settle most often, marine webs less often, and soil (terrestrial belowground) webs in fewer than half of the runs.\nNeutral top consumers are merged in about one community in ten, most of them marine.","title":"The observed webs under the bioenergetic model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#Metric-values:-observed-against-niche-model-webs","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"The niche-model ensemble stores the SI versions of Amax, tmax and the median metrics in its *_si columns. These are the versions comparable with observed webs, which have no niche order. Both tables are prepared as in the paper (sign flips and transforms, so that larger means more stable).\n\nsim = CSV.read(joinpath(data, \"simulated\", \"ensemble_v4.csv\"), DataFrame)\nfor c in (:Amax, :tmax, :MRinf, :MR0, :MAmax, :Mtmax)\n    sim[!, c] = sim[!, Symbol(c, \"_si\")]\nend\nokobs = obs[obs.status .== \"ok\", :]\nniche = prepare_metrics(sim)\nobserved = prepare_metrics(okobs)\nobserved.web = okobs.web\nniche = niche[[all(isfinite, r[METRICS]) for r in eachrow(niche)], :]\nobserved = observed[[all(isfinite, r[METRICS]) for r in eachrow(observed)], :]\n(niche = nrow(niche), observed = nrow(observed), observed_webs = length(unique(observed.web)))\n\n(niche = 10000, observed = 546, observed_webs = 207)\n\npanels = map([\"Rinf\", \"R0\", \"<RE>\", \"<SE>\"]) do m\n    scatter(niche.N, niche[!, m]; ms = 1, msw = 0, alpha = 0.15, color = :grey, label = \"\", title = m,\n            xscale = :log10, xlabel = \"N\", titlefontsize = 10)\n    scatter!(observed.N, observed[!, m]; ms = 2.5, msw = 0, alpha = 0.6, color = :darkorange, label = \"\")\nend\nplot(panels...; layout = (2, 2), size = (750, 550))\n\n(Image: Four metrics against final richness. Grey: niche-model ensemble; orange: observed webs.)\n\nObserved communities are small. Most keep fewer than 30 species, whereas the niche ensemble spans 5–100.\nAt equal richness the values lie inside or at the edge of the niche-model cloud. Observed webs lose somewhat more species after a removal (lower ⟨SE⟩) at intermediate richness, and their reactivity R0 spreads more widely.","title":"Metric values: observed against niche-model webs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#Correlation-structure","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"Observed webs are few at any one richness, so both datasets are binned by final richness (5–15, 16–30, 31–60, 61–120). Spearman correlations are computed within bins with at least 20 communities and averaged over the bins. A metric that is constant in a bin has no defined correlations there and is left out of that bin’s average.\n\nconst BINS = [(5, 15), (16, 30), (31, 60), (61, 120)]\n\nfunction binned(m)\n    b = [something(findfirst(r -> r[1] ≤ n ≤ r[2], BINS), 0) for n in m.N]\n    m = m[b .> 0, :]\n    m.N = b[b .> 0]               # size_correlations groups by N: use the bin index\n    return m\nend\n\nfunction binned_correlations(m; min_n = 20)\n    sc = size_correlations(m; sizes = 1:length(BINS), max_sample = typemax(Int))\n    use = findall(≥(min_n), sc.n)\n    k = length(METRICS)\n    R = [let v = filter(!isnan, sc.rho[i, j, use]); isempty(v) ? 0.0 : mean(v) end for i in 1:k, j in 1:k]\n    return R, sc.n\nend\n\nupper(R) = [R[i, j] for j in axes(R, 2) for i in axes(R, 1) if i < j]\nrand_index(a, b) = mean((a[i] == a[j]) == (b[i] == b[j]) for i in eachindex(a) for j in eachindex(a) if i < j)\n\nBn, Bo = binned(niche), binned(observed)\nRn, nn = binned_correlations(Bn)\nRo, no = binned_correlations(Bo)\ngn, Qn = modular_groups(metric_network(Rn); runs = 50, rng = Xoshiro(1))\ngo, Qo = modular_groups(metric_network(Ro); runs = 50, rng = Xoshiro(1))\nx, y = upper(Rn), upper(Ro)\n(communities_per_bin_niche = nn, communities_per_bin_observed = no,\n correlation_of_pair_correlations = round(cor(x, y); digits = 2), mean_abs_difference = round(mean(abs.(x .- y)); digits = 3),\n rand_index_of_groups = round(rand_index(gn, go); digits = 2))\n\n(communities_per_bin_niche = [1053, 1572, 3048, 4270], communities_per_bin_observed = [282, 99, 63, 46], correlation_of_pair_correlations = 0.81, mean_abs_difference = 0.138, rand_index_of_groups = 0.93)\n\nThe two structures correlate at 0.81. Whether that is a large or a small difference depends on how noisy the observed structure is. Splitting the observed webs into two random halves, with each web’s replicates kept together, gives the noise ceiling: how well the structure agrees with itself.\n\nfunction split_half(m; splits = 20)\n    units = unique(m.web)\n    r, d = Float64[], Float64[]\n    for s in 1:splits\n        half = Set(shuffle(Xoshiro(s), units)[1:(length(units) ÷ 2)])\n        inhalf = in.(m.web, Ref(half))\n        a, b = upper(binned_correlations(m[inhalf, :])[1]), upper(binned_correlations(m[.!inhalf, :])[1])\n        push!(r, cor(a, b))\n        push!(d, mean(abs.(a .- b)))\n    end\n    return (correlation = round(mean(r); digits = 2), range = round.(extrema(r); digits = 2),\n            mean_abs_difference = round(mean(d); digits = 3))\nend\nsplit_half(Bo)\n\n(correlation = 0.85, range = (0.72, 0.92), mean_abs_difference = 0.124)\n\nThe observed webs disagree with each other about as much as they disagree with the niche-model ensemble. At this sample size, changing the topology from niche-model to observed webs does not change the overall correlation structure by more than the variation among observed webs.\n\nThe groups found by Louvain modularity:\n\nfor (name, g, Q) in ((\"niche-model webs\", gn, Qn), (\"observed webs\", go, Qo))\n    @printf(\"%s (Q = %.3f)\\n\", name, Q)\n    foreach(k -> println(\"    \", join(METRICS[g .== k], \" \")), 1:maximum(g))\nend\n\nniche-model webs (Q = 0.204)\n    R0 Is Amax tmax Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n    MR0 RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\n    MAmax Mtmax\nobserved webs (Q = 0.119)\n    R0 MR0 RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\n    Is Amax tmax Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n    MAmax Mtmax\n\nBoth have three groups, and most metrics keep their group (Rand index above).\nMAmax and Mtmax form a group of their own in both. With the SI’s pulse weights they are constant in most bins (see the early-response vignette).\nThe Jacobian and press metrics stay together. The exception is R0, which in observed webs joins MR0 and the removal and sensitivity metrics.\n\nThe pairs that differ most:\n\npairs = [(i, j) for j in eachindex(METRICS) for i in eachindex(METRICS) if i < j]\nΔ = [Ro[i, j] - Rn[i, j] for (i, j) in pairs]\ntop = sortperm(abs.(Δ); rev = true)[1:8]\nDataFrame(pair = [\"$(METRICS[pairs[k][1]])–$(METRICS[pairs[k][2]])\" for k in top],\n          niche = round.([Rn[pairs[k]...] for k in top]; digits = 2),\n          observed = round.([Ro[pairs[k]...] for k in top]; digits = 2))\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">8×3 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow pair niche observed\n String Float64 Float64\n1 \\<RM\\>–\\<RE\\> 0.6 -0.05\n2 \\<RE\\>–\\<SE\\> 0.61 0.07\n3 TM–\\<SE\\> -0.25 0.25\n4 S–\\<SE\\> -0.32 0.14\n5 TM–maxRM -0.04 0.39\n6 MR0–\\<RM\\> 0.12 0.55\n7 \\<CE\\>–\\<SM\\> 0.21 0.63\n8 minTM–\\<SE\\> -0.15 0.26\n\n</div>\n\nThe removal metrics change most. In niche-model webs, robustness to species removal ⟨RE⟩ goes with the other removal and extinction metrics. In observed webs it decouples from them. Secondary extinctions follow network structure, and observed webs differ from niche webs in their basal fraction, detritus and degree distribution.\nSome pairs couple more strongly in observed webs. These are tolerance to a global mortality press (TM) with the removal metrics maxRM and ⟨SE⟩, and ⟨CE⟩ with ⟨SM⟩.\nSo the paper’s “distance to threshold” dimension is the one most specific to the niche model.","title":"Correlation structure"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#Caveats","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"Model masses, not measured ones. Only the topology is observed. The measured masses need a different parameterisation to be viable.\nDetritus is approximated. Non-living resources are basal nodes with logistic renewal, which stands in for donor control.\nFew large observed communities. The 61–120 bin holds few observed communities, so the structure is dominated by small webs.\nThree replicates per web. scripts/observed_webs.jl runs any number with the same seeds (it used five for the results in the project wiki); more replicates narrow the noise ceiling but do not add webs.\nAggregation changes the webs. Trophic species are the fairer comparison with the niche model, but they are not the published nodes.","title":"Caveats"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_observed_food_webs/#References","page":"Observed food webs (GATEWAy)","text":"Brose U. et al. (2006) Allometric scaling enhances stability in complex food webs. Ecology Letters 9:1228–1236.\nBrose U. et al. (2019) Predator traits determine food-web architecture across ecosystems. Nature Ecology & Evolution 3:919–927. Data: GATEWAy v1.0, doi:10.25829/IDIV.283-3-756 (CC BY 4.0).\nDomínguez-García V., Dakos V. & Kéfi S. (2019) Unveiling dimensions of stability in complex ecological networks. PNAS 116:25714–25720.\nWilliams R.J. & Martinez N.D. (2000) Simple rules yield complex food webs. Nature 404:180–183.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_reproducing_the_analysis/#Reproducing-the-analysis-of-Domínguez-García-et-al.-(2019)","page":"Reproducing the analysis","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nCorrelations change with community size\nNetworks of metrics and their groups\nHierarchical clustering\nHow much variance can a few metrics capture?","title":"Reproducing the analysis of Domínguez-García et al. (2019)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_reproducing_the_analysis/#Overview","page":"Reproducing the analysis","text":"Domínguez-García, Dakos and Kéfi (2019, PNAS 116:25714) computed 27 stability metrics for thousands of simulated food webs and asked how they are related. Their answer was three groups of correlated metrics, which they called early response to pulse, sensitivities to press and distance to threshold. This vignette repeats their statistical analysis on their own data (Dataset S2, 100 communities for each richness from 5 to 99 species). Each step is also checked against the output of their Python code. The next vignettes regenerate the data themselves.\n\nusing DominguezGarcia2019\nusing DataFrames, Statistics, Random, Printf\nusing LinearAlgebra: I\nusing Plots\ndata = joinpath(pkgdir(DominguezGarcia2019), \"data\", \"dominguez-garcia2019\")\ndf = read_metrics(joinpath(data, \"Dataset_S2.csv\"))\nsize(df)\n\n(9380, 29)\n\nread_metrics reads the raw columns of the authors’ GitHub deposit. It then applies the transformations of their prepare_dataframe, so that a larger value of each metric means a more stable community. The result equals the publisher’s version of Dataset S2 to within 4e-13.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_reproducing_the_analysis/#Correlations-change-with-community-size","page":"Reproducing the analysis","text":"For each richness, size_correlations computes the 27 × 27 Spearman correlation matrix (and two covariance matrices used later).\n\nsc = size_correlations(df)\nli = Dict(l => i for (i, l) in enumerate(sc.labels))\nρ(a, b) = sc.rho[li[a], li[b], :]\ntrailing(x, k = 6) = [i < k ? NaN : mean(x[(i - k + 1):i]) for i in eachindex(x)]\npairs = [(\"TM\", \"<TM>\"), (\"Is\", \"tmax\"), (\"Rinf\", \"<s_ij>\"), (\"RM\", \"SM\")]\nplot([plot(sc.sizes, ρ(a, b); label = \"per size\", alpha = 0.4, title = \"$a vs $b\", ylim = (-1, 1)) for (a, b) in pairs]...;\n     layout = (2, 2), size = (800, 550), xlabel = \"species richness\", ylabel = \"Spearman ρ\")\nfor (k, (a, b)) in enumerate(pairs)\n    plot!(sc.sizes, trailing(ρ(a, b)); subplot = k, label = \"rolling mean of 6\", lw = 2)\nend\ncurrent()\n\n(Image: )\n\nThese are the four pairs of the paper’s Figure 1. Like the authors’ code, the smoothed curve is a trailing mean of six sizes, although SI §1 says it is centred.\n\nSI §1 classifies each of the 351 pairs by how its correlation differs between small (5–15), medium and large (85–95) communities. The authors’ code uses some windows that differ slightly from the text (5–14 for one test, and 50–59 for “medium”).\n\ntypes = correlation_types(sc)\ncombine(groupby(types, :type), nrow => :pairs)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">7×2 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow type pairs\n String Int64\n1 F 105\n2 C 7\n3 E 64\n4 G 155\n5 D 5\n6 B 8\n7 A 7\n\n</div>\n\nThese are the counts the authors’ code produces: 155 pairs (44%) are unchanged (G), and most of the others weaken (F, 105) or strengthen (E,\n\nbetween small and medium communities, then stay constant.","title":"Correlations change with community size"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_reproducing_the_analysis/#Networks-of-metrics-and-their-groups","page":"Reproducing the analysis","text":"For each size class, the correlations are averaged over the sizes in the class. A network is then built with the metrics as nodes and the absolute mean correlations as edge weights. Modularity measures how well a partition separates strongly connected groups.\n\ngroups_of(g) = [sc.labels[g .== k] for k in 1:maximum(g)]\nresults = map(SIZE_CLASSES) do (name, (lo, hi))\n    R, _ = class_average(sc, lo, hi)\n    W = metric_network(R)\n    g, Q = modular_groups(W; runs = 50, rng = Xoshiro(1))\n    name => (R = R, W = W, groups = g, Q = Q)\nend |> Dict\nfor name in (\"small\", \"medium\", \"large\")\n    r = results[name]\n    @printf(\"%-6s Q = %.4f\\n\", name, r.Q)\n    foreach(m -> println(\"   \", join(m, \" \")), groups_of(r.groups))\nend\n\nsmall  Q = 0.1474\n   R0 Is Amax tmax Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n   MR0 MAmax Mtmax\n   RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\nmedium Q = 0.1767\n   R0 Amax RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\n   Is tmax Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n   MR0 MAmax Mtmax\nlarge  Q = 0.2074\n   R0 Amax tmax MR0 MAmax Mtmax\n   Is Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\n   RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\n\nThe paper reports a best partition with modularity Q = 0.177 from ten Gephi runs. The Louvain method here finds Q = 0.1767 on the medium class, so the paper’s figure most likely refers to that class. The partition shipped with the authors’ code, which is the same file for all three classes, scores a little lower:\n\nshipped = Dict(\"MAmax\" => 0, \"Mtmax\" => 0, \"MR0\" => 0, \"Amax\" => 1, \"R0\" => 1, \"tmax\" => 1,\n               \"RM\" => 3, \"<RM>\" => 3, \"<SE>\" => 3, \"maxSE\" => 3, \"SM\" => 3, \"<SM>\" => 3, \"maxRE\" => 3,\n               \"maxSM\" => 3, \"maxRM\" => 3, \"<RE>\" => 3)\ngship = [get(shipped, l, 2) for l in sc.labels]\n[@sprintf(\"%s: Q(shipped) = %.4f, Q(Louvain) = %.4f\", n, modularity(results[n].W, gship), results[n].Q)\n for n in (\"small\", \"medium\", \"large\")]\n\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"small: Q(shipped) = 0.1303, Q(Louvain) = 0.1474\"\n \"medium: Q(shipped) = 0.1751, Q(Louvain) = 0.1767\"\n \"large: Q(shipped) = 0.2040, Q(Louvain) = 0.2074\"\n\nThe three groups of the paper are clear in every class: the median pulse metrics (MR0, MAmax, Mtmax); the resistance and sensitivity of biomass to presses and extinctions; and resilience, invariability, sensitivity and tolerance. The metrics the paper calls idiosyncratic (R0, Amax, tmax) move between groups as the communities grow.","title":"Networks of metrics and their groups"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_reproducing_the_analysis/#Hierarchical-clustering","page":"Reproducing the analysis","text":"The paper describes average-linkage clustering on the distance d = 1 − ρ. The authors’ code instead clusters the rows of \\|ρ\\| as profiles, using correlation distance. metric_dendrogram does either.\n\nR = results[\"medium\"].R\ndd = metric_dendrogram(R)\norder = dd.tree.order\nheatmap(sc.labels[order], sc.labels[order], R[order, order]; c = :RdBu, clim = (-1, 1),\n        xrotation = 90, size = (700, 620), title = \"Medium communities, ordered by the dendrogram\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nfor (name, (lo, hi)) in SIZE_CLASSES\n    R, _ = class_average(sc, lo, hi)\n    a, b = metric_dendrogram(R), metric_dendrogram(R; distance = :one_minus_rho)\n    @printf(\"%-6s as coded: %.3f (vs its own distance %.3f); with d = 1 − ρ: %.3f\\n\", name, a.c_code, a.c_clustering,\n            b.c_clustering)\nend\n\nsmall  as coded: 0.872 (vs its own distance 0.889); with d = 1 − ρ: 0.859\nlarge  as coded: 0.869 (vs its own distance 0.945); with d = 1 − ρ: 0.911\nmedium as coded: 0.846 (vs its own distance 0.912); with d = 1 − ρ: 0.872\n\nThe coefficient the authors print (as coded) compares the tree with a Euclidean distance it was not built from. Its medium-class value, 0.846, is the c = 0.85 of the paper.","title":"Hierarchical clustering"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_reproducing_the_analysis/#How-much-variance-can-a-few-metrics-capture?","page":"Reproducing the analysis","text":"For each metric, explained_variance gives the share of the total variance of the (mean/range-normalised) metrics that it accounts for through its covariances (SI §6).\n\nΣ, _ = class_average(sc, 45, 55; field = :valuecov)\nev, own = explained_variance(Σ)\nsort(DataFrame(metric = sc.labels, explained = ev, own = own), :explained; rev = true)[1:8, :]\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">8×3 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow metric explained own\n String Float64 Float64\n1 \\<TM\\> 0.2437 0.040346\n2 \\<s_ij\\> 0.237236 0.0319732\n3 Rinf 0.227862 0.0426443\n4 minTM 0.219538 0.0418593\n5 TM 0.211224 0.0507891\n6 S 0.207844 0.0337527\n7 \\<CE\\> 0.207 0.0390786\n8 TE 0.185343 0.0383414\n\n</div>\n\nThese values equal the Python output to 1e-16. The paper’s comparison of three metrics taken from one group with three taken from different groups uses the volume of the rank-covariance ellipsoid (SI §7). The volume is scaled so that independent metrics of equal variance give 1.\n\nΣr, _ = class_average(sc, 45, 55; field = :rankcov)\ng = results[\"medium\"].groups\nvol(ms) = ellipsoid_volume(Σr[ms, ms]) / ellipsoid_volume(Matrix(1.0I, 3, 3))\nrng = Xoshiro(1234)\nbig = [findall(==(k), g) for k in 1:maximum(g) if count(==(k), g) > 3]\nwithin = [mean(vol(randperm(rng, length(G))[1:3] |> p -> G[p]) for _ in 1:2000) for G in big]\nacross = mean(vol([rand(rng, G) for G in big]) for _ in 1:2000)\n(within_groups = round.(within; digits = 3), one_per_group = round(across; digits = 3))\n\n(within_groups = [0.803, 0.565], one_per_group = 1.893)\n\nWith three large groups in the medium class, three metrics from different groups span more of the space than three from one group. This is the paper’s argument for measuring one metric from each group.","title":"How much variance can a few metrics capture?"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_robustness/#Robustness-of-the-reproduction-to-model-settings","page":"Robustness to model settings","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSome settings rarely give a viable community\nThe correlation structure\nWhere the early-response metrics go\nThe early-response group follows the weights\nCaveats\nData","title":"Robustness of the reproduction to model settings"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_robustness/#Overview","page":"Robustness to model settings","text":"The reproduction of Domínguez-García et al. (2019) rests on a reconstructed simulator. Its settings were calibrated to match summaries of the published Dataset S1:\n\nhalf-saturation density h₀ = 0.4;\nfunctional-response exponent 1 + q with q = 0.3;\nno plant metabolism;\nmetabolic exponent −0.25.\n\nTwo conclusions of the reproduction depend on this simulator:\n\nthe correlation structure: simulated metric correlations reproduce the published three groups (an end-to-end ensemble);\nthe early-response artefact: MR0, MAmax and Mtmax form a group only under the published, misaligned pulse weights (the early response to pulse).\n\nThis vignette asks whether either conclusion changes when one setting changes. Each variant uses the same community seeds as the baseline, so differences between them are not due to drawing different webs.\n\nVariant Setting changed Calibrated value\nh0_0.2, h0_0.7 half-saturation density h₀ = 0.2 or 0.7 0.4\nq_0.6, q_0.1 functional-response exponent 1 + q, q = 0.6 or 0.1 0.3\nplant_metab plants pay metabolic costs no\nxexp_+0.25 metabolic exponent +0.25 −0.25\n\nusing DominguezGarcia2019\nusing CSV, DataFrames, Statistics, Printf\nusing Plots\nusing Random: Xoshiro\n\ndata = joinpath(pkgdir(DominguezGarcia2019), \"data\")\npub = read_metrics(joinpath(data, \"dominguez-garcia2019\", \"Dataset_S2.csv\"))\n\n# the baseline: the first 1,200 seeds of the calibrated ensemble, the seeds every variant used\nbase = CSV.read(joinpath(data, \"simulated\", \"ensemble_v4.csv\"), DataFrame)\nbase = base[in.(base.network, Ref(Set(\"Sim$(20191217 + k)\" for k in 1:1200))), :]\nfiles = [\"h0_0.2\" => \"h0_0.2\", \"h0_0.7\" => \"h0_0.7\", \"q_0.6\" => \"q_0.6\",\n         \"plant_metab\" => \"plant_metabolism\", \"q_0.1\" => \"q_0.1\",\n         \"xexp_+0.25\" => \"metabolic_exponent_+0.25\"]\nensembles = [\"baseline\" => base;\n             [name => CSV.read(joinpath(data, \"simulated\", \"robustness\", f * \".csv\"), DataFrame)\n              for (name, f) in files]]\nensembles = [name => d[5 .<= d.N .<= 99, :] for (name, d) in ensembles]\nDataFrame(variant = first.(ensembles), communities = nrow.(last.(ensembles)))\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">7×2 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow variant communities\n String Int64\n1 baseline 1165\n2 h0_0.2 1164\n3 h0_0.7 93\n4 q_0.6 1173\n5 plant_metab 1158\n6 q_0.1 6\n7 xexp_+0.25 9\n\n</div>","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_robustness/#Some-settings-rarely-give-a-viable-community","page":"Robustness to model settings","text":"A community is kept only if it reaches a locally stable equilibrium that keeps at least 90% of its species. The number of assembly draws needed per viable community measures how open a setting is to that rule. Three variants (h₀ = 0.7, q = 0.1 and an exponent of +0.25) were stopped after a 90-minute budget, which explains their small samples.\n\nviability = DataFrame(variant = first.(ensembles),\n                      communities = nrow.(last.(ensembles)),\n                      draws_per_community = [round(mean(d.attempts); digits = 1) for (_, d) in ensembles],\n                      species_kept = [round(median(d.N ./ d.iniN); digits = 3) for (_, d) in ensembles],\n                      plant_share = [round(median(d.Nb ./ d.N); digits = 3) for (_, d) in ensembles])\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">7×5 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow variant communities drawspercommunity species_kept plant_share\n String Int64 Float64 Float64 Float64\n1 baseline 1165 23.7 0.921 0.189\n2 h0_0.2 1164 6.7 0.929 0.172\n3 h0_0.7 93 304.8 0.917 0.231\n4 q_0.6 1173 21.8 0.923 0.189\n5 plant_metab 1158 49.5 0.917 0.202\n6 q_0.1 6 3171.7 0.896 0.298\n7 xexp_+0.25 9 539.4 0.915 0.273\n\n</div>\n\nbar(viability.variant, viability.draws_per_community; yscale = :log10, legend = false,\n    ylabel = \"draws per viable community\", xrotation = 30, color = :steelblue,\n    size = (700, 380), bottom_margin = 6Plots.mm, left_margin = 4Plots.mm)\n\n<div id=\"fig-draws\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: Assembly draws per viable community (log scale), on the same 1,200 seeds.\n\n</div>\n\nh₀ = 0.2 makes assembly easier: about 7 draws per community, against 24 for the baseline.\nPlant metabolism doubles the draws, and h₀ = 0.7 needs about 13 times as many.\nq = 0.1 and an exponent of +0.25 almost never give a viable community: thousands and hundreds of draws each, so only 6 and 9 communities were reached in the budget. The paper’s assembly rule itself largely closes these parts of parameter space.","title":"Some settings rarely give a viable community"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_robustness/#The-correlation-structure","page":"Robustness to model settings","text":"As in the comparison of structures across datasets, Spearman correlations are computed within four richness bins (5–15, 16–30, 31–60 and 61–99 species) and averaged. The metric network is then partitioned by Louvain modularity. A coarser binning than the paper’s size classes is needed because each variant has at most 1,200 communities.\n\nconst BINS = [(5, 15), (16, 30), (31, 60), (61, 99)]\nconst SIX = (:Amax, :tmax, :MRinf, :MR0, :MAmax, :Mtmax)   # the metrics with an SI version\n\nfunction structure(m)\n    b = [something(findfirst(r -> r[1] ≤ n ≤ r[2], BINS), 0) for n in m.N]\n    m = m[b .> 0, :]\n    m.N = b[b .> 0]\n    sc = size_correlations(m; sizes = 1:length(BINS), max_sample = typemax(Int))\n    k = length(sc.labels)\n    R = [let v = filter(!isnan, sc.rho[i, j, :])\n             isempty(v) ? NaN : mean(v)\n         end for i in 1:k, j in 1:k]\n    g, Q = modular_groups(metric_network(R); runs = 50, rng = Xoshiro(1))\n    return (R = R, labels = sc.labels, groups = g, Q = Q)\nend\n\n# the metrics of one ensemble under the published or the SI's median-metric weights\nfunction metrics(d, weights)\n    d = copy(d)\n    weights == \"SI\" && foreach(c -> (d[!, c] = d[!, Symbol(c, \"_si\")]), SIX)\n    return prepare_metrics(d)\nend\n\nref = structure(pub)\nfor k in 1:maximum(ref.groups)\n    println(\"published group $k: \", join(ref.labels[ref.groups .== k], \" \"))\nend\n\npublished group 1: R0 Is Amax tmax Rinf MRinf S <s_ij> TM <TM> minTM <CE> TE maxCE\npublished group 2: MR0 MAmax Mtmax\npublished group 3: RM <RM> maxRM SM <SM> maxSM <RE> maxRE <SE> maxSE\n\nEach simulated structure is compared with the published one and with the baseline:\n\nr(ρ): the correlation between the 351 pairwise metric correlations;\nmean \\|Δρ\\|: their mean absolute difference;\nRand: the agreement of the two partitions;\nearly: whether MR0, MAmax and Mtmax fall in one group.\n\nupper(R) = [R[i, j] for j in axes(R, 2) for i in axes(R, 1) if i < j]\nfunction agreement(a, b)\n    x, y = upper(a.R), upper(b.R)\n    ok = isfinite.(x) .& isfinite.(y)\n    return cor(x[ok], y[ok]), mean(abs.(x[ok] .- y[ok]))\nend\nrand_index(a, b) = mean((a[i] == a[j]) == (b[i] == b[j]) for i in eachindex(a) for j in eachindex(a) if i < j)\nearly(s) = length(unique(s.groups[[findfirst(==(l), s.labels) for l in (\"MR0\", \"MAmax\", \"Mtmax\")]])) == 1\n\nstructures = Dict((name, w) => structure(metrics(d, w)) for (name, d) in ensembles, w in (\"published\", \"SI\"))\nrows = map(Iterators.product((\"published\", \"SI\"), first.(ensembles))) do (w, name)\n    s = structures[(name, w)]\n    d = Dict(ensembles)[name]\n    r, Δ = agreement(ref, s)\n    rb, _ = agreement(structures[(\"baseline\", w)], s)\n    (variant = name, weights = w, amplify = round(100mean(d[!, w == \"SI\" ? :MAmax_si : :MAmax] .> 1 + 1e-12); digits = 1),\n     r_published = round(r; digits = 2), mean_abs_diff = round(Δ; digits = 3),\n     rand_published = round(rand_index(ref.groups, s.groups); digits = 2),\n     r_baseline = round(rb; digits = 2), groups = maximum(s.groups), early = early(s))\nend\ntable = DataFrame(vec(rows))\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">14×9 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow variant weights amplify r_published meanabsdiff rand_published r_baseline groups early\n String String Float64 Float64 Float64 Float64 Float64 Int64 Bool\n1 baseline published 20.9 0.98 0.035 0.91 1.0 3 true\n2 baseline SI 0.1 0.9 0.071 0.97 1.0 3 false\n3 h0_0.2 published 21.2 0.97 0.053 1.0 0.97 3 true\n4 h0_0.2 SI 0.0 0.92 0.075 0.89 0.96 4 false\n5 h0_0.7 published 18.3 0.85 0.111 0.85 0.88 3 true\n6 h0_0.7 SI 0.0 0.8 0.129 0.96 0.86 4 false\n7 q_0.6 published 4.5 0.96 0.064 0.91 0.96 3 true\n8 q_0.6 SI 0.0 0.93 0.083 0.96 0.96 4 false\n9 plant_metab published 21.3 0.99 0.035 1.0 0.99 3 true\n10 plant_metab SI 0.1 0.91 0.07 0.97 0.99 3 false\n11 q_0.1 published 66.7 0.5 0.453 0.52 0.49 2 true\n12 q_0.1 SI 0.0 0.43 0.473 0.6 0.45 5 false\n13 xexp_+0.25 published 22.2 0.58 0.388 0.5 0.55 2 true\n14 xexp_+0.25 SI 0.0 0.57 0.402 0.55 0.55 4 false\n\n</div>\n\namplify is the percentage of communities whose median pulse is amplified (MAmax \\> 1).\n\nearly_metric = [l in (\"MR0\", \"MAmax\", \"Mtmax\") for l in ref.labels]\npair_early = [early_metric[i] || early_metric[j] for j in eachindex(ref.labels) for i in eachindex(ref.labels) if i < j]\npanels = map([\"baseline\", \"h0_0.2\", \"q_0.6\", \"plant_metab\"]) do name\n    y = upper(structures[(name, \"published\")].R)\n    x = upper(ref.R)\n    p = scatter(x[.!pair_early], y[.!pair_early]; ms = 2.5, msw = 0, alpha = 0.6, color = :steelblue,\n                title = name, titlefontsize = 10, legend = false, xlims = (-1, 1), ylims = (-1, 1))\n    scatter!(p, x[pair_early], y[pair_early]; ms = 3, msw = 0, color = :darkorange)\n    plot!(p, [-1, 1], [-1, 1]; color = :gray, ls = :dash)\nend\nplot(panels...; layout = (2, 2), size = (700, 640), xlabel = \"published ρ\", ylabel = \"simulated ρ\",\n     guidefontsize = 8)\n\n<div id=\"fig-scatter\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: Pairwise metric correlations in four well-sampled variants against the published ones, under the published weights. Orange: pairs involving MR0, MAmax or Mtmax.\n\n</div>\n\nWhere a variant can be sampled, the structure holds. h₀ = 0.2, q = 0.6 and plant metabolism each give over 1,150 communities. Under the published weights they reproduce the published correlations about as well as the baseline does (r(ρ) 0.96–0.99, against 0.98). Each gives three groups close to the published ones (Rand 0.91–1.00).\nThey also agree with the baseline on the same seeds: r_baseline is 0.96–0.99 under the published weights. Changing these settings moves the metrics, not their correlations.\nh₀ = 0.7 (93 communities) still finds three groups, with a weaker fit to the published correlations (r(ρ) 0.85, and 0.88 against the baseline), as expected from a twelfth of the sample.","title":"The correlation structure"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_robustness/#Where-the-early-response-metrics-go","page":"Robustness to model settings","text":"For each partition, the table lists the metrics grouped with MAmax, and says where MR0 sits: with MAmax, with the sensitivities to press (the group holding RM), or elsewhere.\n\nmembers(s, l) = s.labels[s.groups .== s.groups[findfirst(==(l), s.labels)]]\nfunction mr0_with(s)\n    m = members(s, \"MR0\")\n    \"MAmax\" in m && return \"MAmax\"\n    \"RM\" in m && return \"press sensitivities\"\n    return join(filter(!=(\"MR0\"), m), \" \")\nend\nDataFrame(variant = table.variant, weights = table.weights,\n          with_MAmax = [join(filter(!=(\"MAmax\"), members(structures[(r.variant, r.weights)], \"MAmax\")), \" \")\n                        for r in eachrow(table)],\n          MR0_with = [mr0_with(structures[(r.variant, r.weights)]) for r in eachrow(table)])\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">14×4 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow variant weights with_MAmax MR0_with\n String String String String\n1 baseline published R0 Amax MR0 Mtmax MAmax\n2 baseline SI Mtmax press sensitivities\n3 h0_0.2 published MR0 Mtmax MAmax\n4 h0_0.2 SI  press sensitivities\n5 h0_0.7 published R0 Amax MR0 Mtmax MAmax\n6 h0_0.7 SI  press sensitivities\n7 q_0.6 published R0 Amax MR0 Mtmax MAmax\n8 q_0.6 SI  press sensitivities\n9 plant_metab published MR0 Mtmax MAmax\n10 plant_metab SI Mtmax press sensitivities\n11 q_0.1 published R0 Is tmax MR0 Mtmax \\<s_ij\\> TM \\<CE\\> maxCE \\<SM\\> maxSM maxSE MAmax\n12 q_0.1 SI  \\<s_ij\\> \\<SM\\> \\<SE\\> maxSE\n13 xexp_+0.25 published R0 Is Amax tmax MR0 Mtmax S \\<s_ij\\> \\<CE\\> RM maxRM SM maxSE MAmax\n14 xexp_+0.25 SI  press sensitivities\n\n</div>\n\nUnder the published weights MR0, MAmax and Mtmax share a group in every variant. h₀ = 0.2 and plant metabolism give exactly the published partition. In the baseline, q = 0.6 and h₀ = 0.7, the extremal pulse metrics R0 and Amax join the group, much as in the large communities of the early-response vignette. With h₀ = 0.7, tmax also moves to the press group.\nUnder the SI’s weights the group breaks up the same way in every well-sampled variant. MAmax and Mtmax are left alone or as a pair, and MR0 joins the sensitivities to press. With almost no amplified median pulse, MAmax and Mtmax are nearly constant across communities, so they correlate with nothing.","title":"Where the early-response metrics go"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_robustness/#The-early-response-group-follows-the-weights","page":"Robustness to model settings","text":"pubw = table[table.weights .== \"published\", :]\nsiw = table[table.weights .== \"SI\", :]\nx = 1:nrow(pubw)\nbar(x .- 0.2, pubw.amplify; bar_width = 0.4, label = \"published weights\", color = :darkorange,\n    xticks = (x, pubw.variant), ylabel = \"% with MAmax > 1\", xrotation = 30, size = (700, 380),\n    bottom_margin = 6Plots.mm, left_margin = 4Plots.mm)\nbar!(x .+ 0.2, siw.amplify; bar_width = 0.4, label = \"SI weights\", color = :steelblue)\n\n<div id=\"fig-amplify\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 3: Share of communities that amplify a median pulse, under the published and the SI’s pulse weights.\n\n</div>\n\nIn every variant the group forms under the published weights and not under the SI’s. The SI’s weights leave 0–0.1% of communities amplifying a median pulse. This extends the early-response artefact from the calibrated model to every setting tried.\nAmplification depends on q; the group does not. With q = 0.6 only 4.5% of communities amplify under the published weights, against 21% in the baseline, yet the group still forms. The misaligned weights still make MAmax and Mtmax vary between communities.\nThe two barely sampled variants say little. With q = 0.1 and an exponent of +0.25, 6 and 9 communities give partitions with two groups and correlations far from the published ones (r(ρ) 0.50 and 0.58). That MR0, MAmax and Mtmax share a group there is unsurprising when 27 metrics are split in two, and is not evidence either way.","title":"The early-response group follows the weights"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_robustness/#Caveats","page":"Robustness to model settings","text":"One setting at a time. Interactions between settings are untested.\nThe SI rows are compared with a structure that contains the artefact. Their lower r(ρ) against the published correlations is expected and is not a failure of the variant.\nThe binning is coarser than the paper’s. Four richness bins replace the paper’s three size classes because the variants have 1,200 communities, not 10,000.\nBudgets, not convergence, set the small samples. The h₀ = 0.7, q = 0.1 and +0.25 runs were stopped after 90 minutes; their communities are those reached in that time, so their composition may differ from a full run’s.","title":"Caveats"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_robustness/#Data","page":"Robustness to model settings","text":"The variant ensembles are in data/simulated/robustness/ (see data/simulated/README.md). They were simulated with scripts/simulate_dataset.jl and the setting given as KEY=VALUE, and the summary in this vignette can be printed with scripts/robustness.jl.","title":"Data"},{"category":"section","location":"#DominguezGarcia2019.jl","page":"Home","text":"A reproduction of Domínguez-García, Dakos & Kéfi (2019), \"Unveiling dimensions of stability in complex ecological networks\", PNAS 116:25714 (doi:10.1073/pnas.1904470116).\n\nThe paper computes 27 stability metrics on simulated niche-model food webs, correlates them within each species richness, and groups them into three \"dimensions\" of stability. This package reproduces it end to end, from the simulator, which is not public and was rebuilt from the SI, to the published groups, and then asks how far the conclusions carry.\n\nIt is a case study. It holds only what is specific to the paper, and takes everything else from the ecorecipes packages:\n\nPackage Provides\nResilienceCore.jl the metrics (resilience, reactivity, amplification, median metrics, invariability, sensitivities) and the press and extinction experiments; re-exported here\nFoodWebDynamics.jl the niche model, the allometric bioenergetic model, community assembly and the GATEWAy reader\nCommunityMatrices.jl interaction matrices, structural stability and sign patterns, which began in this package\nDynamicalResilience.jl resilience of SciML models: steady states, thresholds, recovery and basin stability\n\nWhat is here:\n\nConventions: community_record computes the 39 raw columns of Dataset S1 for an assembled community. Two of them follow what the published data did rather than what the SI says: the ∞-norm for Amax and tmax, and misaligned pulse weights for the median metrics. Both can be switched to the SI's definitions.\nDataset transforms: prepare_metrics and read_metrics turn raw columns into the paper's 27 \"larger is more stable\" metrics, as the authors' code does.\nAnalysis: per-size Spearman correlations, size-class averages, networks of metrics and their modular groups, hierarchical clustering, explained variance and covariance ellipsoids. They are checked against the authors' Python code.\nData: the authors' Dataset S2, a 10,000-community ensemble simulated with the reconstructed model, and ensembles under alternative model settings.\n\nThe tutorials reproduce the analysis on the authors' data, follow one food web through all 27 metrics, compare a simulated ensemble with the published one, show that the \"early response to pulse\" dimension is an indexing artefact, run the pipeline on observed food webs, and test the conclusions against changes to the model's settings.","title":"DominguezGarcia2019.jl"},{"category":"module","location":"#DominguezGarcia2019","page":"Home","text":"DominguezGarcia2019\n\nA reproduction of Domínguez-García, Dakos & Kéfi (2019), \"Unveiling dimensions of stability in complex ecological networks\", PNAS 116:25714 (doi:10.1073/pnas.1904470116).\n\nThis is a case study: it holds only what is specific to the paper.\n\ncommunity_record computes the 39 raw columns of the paper's Dataset S1 for an assembled food web, with the conventions the published data actually used (amplification_metrics, published_pulse_covariance, published_max_amplification).\nprepare_metrics and read_metrics turn raw columns into the paper's 27 \"larger is more stable\" metrics.\nThe analysis functions (size_correlations, modular_groups, explained_variance, …) port the authors' Python code.\n\nThe metrics themselves come from ResilienceCore.jl, on a ContinuousJacobian (see community_jacobian), and are re-exported. The food webs, the bioenergetic model and community assembly come from FoodWebDynamics.jl. Interaction matrices, structural stability and sign patterns are in CommunityMatrices.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DominguezGarcia2019"}]
}
